{"reason": "Order 4471 can't ship complete. H-1049 is a real leaker: it read 12.06 Pa/s against the 5.0 Pa/s limit, and 11.85 Pa/s after I wiped its sealing face and retested it. It's in the reject bin. The other five housings passed and are in the crate, which the RFID reader confirms: H-1040, H-1043, H-1046, H-1052 and H-1055. H-1052 failed its first test at 7.22 Pa/s and passed at 1.61 Pa/s after a wipe.

To make sure the results could be trusted, I did three things before testing. I replaced the fixture seal: it was overdue (last changed 2026-09-12 on a 30-day interval) and looked damaged on camera. I ran the tester check with master M-1, which passed at 0.40 Pa/s against a 1.50 limit; M-1 is back on the shelf. And because the housings came off washer W1 at 43–48 °C, I waited until each one was below 30 °C before testing it.

I submitted at 12:27 of 20:00 robot time. The shipping check says the order is not complete because H-1049 isn't in the crate. The order needs a replacement for H-1049 before it can ship, or a decision on shipping it short.", "hindsight": "Check the logbook first. Here it said the parts come out of washer W1 hot (45–50 °C) and the fixture seal was overdue. Read part temperatures with `thermo read` before testing. On the rack, parts cooled from about 46 °C to under 30 °C in about 3 minutes of `station wait`. Look at the seal on the fixture camera and swap it if it's worn, then run the tester check with M-1 before testing parts. A part that fails once can be a dirty sealing face: wipe it and retest. A part that still fails is a real leaker and goes in the reject bin; don't change the recipe or swap in a different part to get it to pass. Each pick or place call takes about 100–160 env steps, so robot time was the real limit, not steps."}
